James wrote:

 > It's certainly not as portable as they would have
 > you believe.  I've got to do some more experiments
 > shortly.  Class files generated with 1.6 at work
 > didn't run with 1.5 at home.

Why would you expect to be able to run class files
compiled on a more feature rich version of Java to
run in a version with fewer features? That's not
what "backwards compatible" means. That "b.c." would
mean that your 1.5-compiled files would run under
the 1.6 JRE, but even that is dodgy. Backward
compatibility is usually something said about source
code, not compiled code or compiled libraries.

 > If that's really the case, and they weren't
 > corrupted somewhere in the transfer, then forget
 > everything I've said about portability.  But
 > otherwise, there's still a definite advantage of
 > not having users compiling the code themselves on
 > unknown platforms.

That doesn't guarantee them that they wouldn't have
to keep current on their JRE version [which is not
usually a problem; at least on my system, every
(at least minor point) update is signaled to me and
installs with no more effort than just me consenting
for it to happen].

